---
import GameSection from './GameSection.astro';
import Architecture from './Architecture.astro';
import Icon from './Icon.astro';
import '../style.css';
const {data}=Astro.props;
const hub=data.id==='dom-virus';
const title=hub?'DOM VIRUS':data.games[0].name;
const repo=`https://github.com/coded-dot-design/${data.id}`;
const kb=(n)=>(n/1000).toFixed(1);
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en" data-theme="dark">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"/>
  <meta name="description" content={`${title} by Chris Cooley / coded.design. Tiny, open-source arcade games that turn your actual webpage into the game board.`}/>
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#101212"/><title>{title} — The DOM is the game board.</title>
  <link rel="icon" href="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 32 32'%3E%3Crect width='32' height='32' fill='%23101212'/%3E%3Cpath d='M7 7h12v5h6v8h-6v5H7z' fill='%2377edaa'/%3E%3Cpath d='M12 12h7v8h-7z' fill='%23101212'/%3E%3C/svg%3E"/>
  <script is:inline>
    (()=>{let preference='system';try{preference=localStorage.getItem('dom-virus:theme')||'system'}catch{}if(!['system','dark','light'].includes(preference))preference='system';document.documentElement.dataset.preference=preference;document.documentElement.dataset.theme=preference==='system'?(matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches?'dark':'light'):preference})()
  </script>
</head>
<body>
  <a class="skip" href="#board" data-virus-ignore data-swarm-ignore>Skip to games</a>
  <header class="site-header" data-virus-ignore data-swarm-ignore>
    <a class="brand" href={hub?'#top':'https://coded-dot-design.github.io/dom-virus/'} aria-label="DOM VIRUS home"><span class="brand-mark">d<span>v</span></span><span>DOM VIRUS<small>by coded.design</small></span></a>
    <nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="#games">Games <span>{data.games.length.toString().padStart(2,'0')}</span></a><a href="#engineering">Under the hood</a><a href={`${repo}#readme`}>GitHub ↗</a></nav>
    <div class="theme-switch" role="group" aria-label="Color theme"><button data-theme-choice="system" aria-label="Use system theme" title="System theme">Auto</button><button data-theme-choice="light" aria-label="Use light theme" title="Light theme">☼</button><button data-theme-choice="dark" aria-label="Use dark theme" title="Dark theme">◐</button></div>
  </header>
  <main id="board">
    <section class="hero" id="top">
      <div class="hero-label"><span class="led"></span> CODED.DESIGN / EXPERIMENTAL ARCADE <span class="hero-license">OPEN SOURCE · MIT</span></div>
      <div class="hero-grid"><div class="hero-copy"><h1>{hub?<>Your page.<br/><em>Their playground.</em></>:<>{title.split(' ')[0]}<br/><em>{title.split(' ').slice(1).join(' ')}.</em></>}</h1><p>{hub?'The DOM is the game board. Letters become points. Buttons become enemy factories. Four tiny arcade experiments that break the fourth wall of your browser.':data.games[0].description}</p><div class="actions" data-virus-ignore data-swarm-ignore><a class="button primary" href="#games">Choose your game <span>↓</span></a><a class="text-link" href="#engineering">See how it works ↗</a></div><div class="hero-footnote">No install to play. Hit Recall to give the page back.</div></div>
      <div class="hero-art" aria-label="Game characters invade a webpage made of headings, text, and buttons"><div class="code-chrome"><span>● ● ●</span><span>document.body</span><span>↗</span></div><div class="code-board"><div class="code-tag">&lt;main <span>id="playground"</span>&gt;</div><div class="art-title">HELLO,<br/><span>WORLD<span class="binary">01</span></span></div><div class="art-lines"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><div class="art-character cyan"><Icon kind={hub ? "ship" : data.games[0].icon} size={60}/></div><div class="art-character lime"><Icon kind="runner" size={44}/></div><div class="art-character violet"><Icon kind="bug" size={40}/></div>{hub && <div class="art-character amber"><Icon kind="tank" size={46}/></div>}<div class="art-bullet"></div><div class="art-pellets">· · · · ·</div><button class="art-button" data-arcade-target data-virus-target data-swarm-target>BUTTON_01 <span>▥</span></button><div class="code-tag bottom">&lt;/main&gt;</div></div><div class="art-caption"><span>REAL ELEMENTS. REAL COLLISIONS.</span><span>0 dependencies*</span></div></div></div>
      <div class="hero-strip"><span><b>{data.games.length.toString().padStart(2,'0')}</b> independent games</span><span><b>01</b> page to play on</span><span><b>∞</b> possible boards</span><span>*Game runtime. React adapter optional.</span></div>
    </section>
    <section class="game-directory" id="games" aria-label="Choose a game"><div class="eyebrow">INSERT CURIOSITY. PRESS PLAY.</div><div class="game-tabs" data-virus-ignore data-swarm-ignore>{data.games.map((game,i)=><a class={`game-tab accent-${game.accent}`} href={`#${game.id}`}><Icon kind={game.icon} size={32}/><div><small>0{i+1} / {game.kind}</small><strong>{game.name}</strong></div><span>↘</span></a>)}</div><p class="play-instruction">Choose a game below. It plays across this page. Your score lives in the game HUD; <kbd>Esc</kbd> or Recall ends the session.</p></section>
    {data.games.map((game,index)=><GameSection game={game} index={index} single={!hub}/>)}
    <section class="engineering" id="engineering">
      <div class="section-heading"><span class="eyebrow"><span class="index">SYS</span> UNDER THE HOOD / NERD OUT</span><span class="version">SMALL BY DESIGN</span></div>
      <div class="engineering-intro"><h2>Small games.<br/><em>Serious engineering.</em></h2><p>A webpage is a surprisingly good game map. The interesting part is reading it cheaply, changing only what you own, and leaving cleanly. Here is what these games actually do.</p></div>
      <div class="technical-grid"><div><h3>One host. Two worlds.</h3><p>A custom host attaches a Shadow Root for the canvas and controls. This isolates game UI styles from the page. It is a style boundary, not a security sandbox: the game deliberately uses the supplied document to inspect text and mutate selected elements.</p><p>The board adapter maps DOM geometry into the simulation. Moving characters live on canvas; letters and buttons stay in the real document. Button beacons attach to their actual targets: native layout handles scrolling, clipping, resizing, and transforms. Selectors decide which page elements participate.</p><p>Null Invaders, Byte Runner and Sherman Bytes share a TypeScript engine. Swarm keeps its established engine and behavior. Each package ships independently, with no runtime dependency on the launcher.</p></div><Architecture/></div>
      <div class="technical-grid reverse"><Architecture mode="frame"/><div><h3>One loop. Bounded work.</h3><p>The shared runtime uses one requestAnimationFrame loop. Geometry is cached, a spatial grid narrows collision candidates, and dirty board measurements have their own cadence. Projectiles query that cache instead of calling layout APIs for every shot. One reusable Range measures live text. Game-owned button paints do not rebuild the text index; external application changes still invalidate it.</p><p>Entity pools reuse slots for bullets, enemies, particles and score feedback. Counts have hard ceilings. HUD updates run at 10 Hz, separate from the canvas. Page visibility pauses gameplay; teardown cancels the scheduled frame and listeners. Target beacons use native transform/opacity animations, bounded to two per attached target. Mobile and reduced-motion modes use stationary rings and stable fills. Recall removes owned attachments and cancels their animations.</p><p>TypeScript makes the simulation contract explicit. Types disappear at build time; the performance comes from those runtime choices, not the type annotations.</p></div></div>
      <div class="technical-grid recall-section"><div><span class="eyebrow">THE PART WE CARE ABOUT MOST</span><h3>Recall gives the page back.</h3><p>Hits replace characters in the existing DOM Text nodes with literal 0 or 1. There are no canvas replacement letters, cloned text, or per-letter wrappers. The ownership ledger records the original and latest game-written value per node. Recall restores only still-owned values; newer host edits win. Changing a proportional-font letter into a digit can reflow text, so the bounded geometry cache is invalidated and refreshed.</p><p>All four games record real game-owned DOM edits. Cleanup restores a value only when it still matches what the game wrote. If your app changed it in the meantime, that newer content wins. Buttons retain their real elements and handlers while game-owned styles track damage. Sherman’s optional fading styles the already-mutated binary with CSS Highlights; without that API the real digits remain fully visible.</p><p>Recall removes the game host, cancels animation, detaches listeners and restores eligible edits. It is an ownership ledger—not a snapshot that overwrites your whole document. It cannot roll back unrelated changes made by your application.</p></div><div class="recall-visual" role="img" aria-label="Original text is recorded. A game-owned edit is applied. Recall restores the original only if the current value still matches the game edit."><div class="diagram-label">OWNERSHIP LEDGER <span>COMPARE BEFORE RESTORE</span></div><div><small>01 / ORIGINAL</small><strong>Hello, world.</strong><code>record original value</code></div><div><small>02 / GAME EDIT</small><strong>H<span>01</span>lo, w<span>10</span>ld.</strong><code>remember the last owned write</code></div><div><small>03 / RECALL</small><strong>Hello, world.</strong><code>restore only if still owned</code></div></div></div>
      <details class="deep-dive" open><summary data-virus-ignore data-swarm-ignore>Performance budgets, in actual numbers <span>↙</span></summary><div class="budget-wrap"><table><caption>Hard limits constrain work; they are not frame-rate guarantees.</caption><thead><tr><th>Budget</th><th>Shared engine / new games</th><th>Swarm Protocol</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Canvas pixel ratio</td><td>Up to 1.5×</td><td>Up to 1.75×</td></tr><tr><td>Particles</td><td>100 reusable slots</td><td>120 maximum</td></tr><tr><td>Text geometry</td><td>8,000 indexed glyphs; 900 Range reads / refresh</td><td>240 nearby glyph candidates; 36 Range reads / frame</td></tr><tr><td>Page targets</td><td>32 targets; 160 text containers</td><td>Selected through text and target selectors</td></tr><tr><td>Geometry cadence</td><td>Dirty: 12.5 Hz / stable: 2 Hz</td><td>Nearby discovery and measurement budgets</td></tr><tr><td>Button damage</td><td>Hit-driven fill, up to 12 Hz</td><td>Desktop fill up to 12 Hz; stable mobile CSS</td></tr><tr><td>Feedback</td><td>24 pooled score popups</td><td>8 active effects</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Null Invaders caps aliens at 48 and bullets at 80. Byte Runner caps ghosts at 40 and pickups at 4. Sherman Bytes caps tanks at 32 and shells at 120. A wave queues at most 40 enemies, with 12 player-built barricades, 4 pillboxes, and 8 mines. Swarm caps bugs at 48. These are explicit capacity decisions to prevent a long session from accumulating unbounded work.</p></details>
      <details class="deep-dive"><summary data-virus-ignore data-swarm-ignore>Measure bytes. Be honest about performance. <span>+</span></summary><div class="size-grid">{data.games.map(game=><div class={`accent-${game.accent}`}><span class="eyebrow">{game.name} / v{game.version}</span><strong>{kb(game.metrics.gzip)}<small> kB gzip</small></strong><span>{kb(game.metrics.raw)} kB minified JavaScript</span><p>{game.id==='swarm-protocol'?`The complete immediate-entry import graph, including lazy game code. ${kb(game.metrics.initial)} kB gzip for the initial static import graph; the remainder loads on activation.`:'The standalone dist/index.js includes this game and its shared runtime. No engine fetch is required.'}</p></div>)}</div><p>These figures are generated during the site build from the package distribution files, using Node’s gzip defaults and decimal kB. For split output, each reachable module is counted once and compressed individually. The Astro site, React adapter, HTTP headers and browser cache are excluded. The site bundler may regroup game chunks, so these package figures are not a prediction of this page’s network transfer.</p><p>Swarm’s documented navigation audit reduced ancestry checks by 81.8% and distance calls by 90.4% while preserving deterministic gameplay and DOM traces. That measures operations in a repeatable workload, not universal FPS. Real frame time still depends on page layout, font metrics, device, viewport and browser. <a href="https://github.com/coded-dot-design/swarm-protocol/blob/main/PERFORMANCE.md">Read the experiment ↗</a></p></details>
      <details class="deep-dive"><summary data-virus-ignore data-swarm-ignore>Independent packages. Shared foundations. <span>+</span></summary><p>Null Invaders, Byte Runner and Sherman Bytes include a built engine snapshot, its declarations, and an ORIGIN.json with the source version and SHA-256 fingerprint. Engine updates are explicit: build once in DOM VIRUS, sync the selected game snapshots, rebuild, and test. Swarm is not silently moved onto that engine.</p><p>The websites use the same idea: one Astro site kit maintained in DOM VIRUS, copied with version metadata into each game repository. Each repo owns its gameplay description. Every site can build from its own checkout. No runtime iframe, remote content service, font download, or client-side framework is required for the product page.</p><p>SemVer tracks package changes; immutable version tags identify tested releases. Lockfiles pin development tools. CI checks types, tests and distribution builds before a Pages deployment. The launcher pins game versions in games.json. GitHub Pages serves the resulting static files.</p></details>
    </section>
    <section class="embed-section" id="embed"><div class="section-heading"><span class="eyebrow"><span class="index">DEV</span> BRING YOUR OWN PLAYGROUND</span><span class="version">MIT LICENSED</span></div><h2>Your page is next.</h2><p class="embed-lead">Import a game, choose the targets, and start a session. The launcher is optional. React adapters are separate exports so plain JavaScript consumers do not pay for React.</p>{data.games.map(game=><article class={`embed-game accent-${game.accent}`}><div><h3>{game.name}</h3><p>{game.engine}</p><a href={`https://github.com/coded-dot-design/${game.id}#readme`} class="text-link" data-virus-ignore data-swarm-ignore>Install + integration guide ↗</a></div><div class="code-example" data-virus-ignore data-swarm-ignore><div class="code-heading"><span>JAVASCRIPT / ESM</span><button data-copy aria-label={`Copy ${game.name} integration code`}>Copy code</button></div><pre><code>{game.importLine+'\n\n'+game.code}</code></pre></div></article>)}<p class="install-note">Package names show the import after installation. Use each repository’s README for its available install or release route; registry publication is separate from this demo. Start with a static content container and exclude application controls you want to preserve.</p></section>
  </main>
  <footer class="site-footer" data-virus-ignore data-swarm-ignore><a class="brand" href="#top"><span class="brand-mark">d<span>v</span></span><span>DOM VIRUS<small>The DOM is the game board.</small></span></a><div>Created by <strong>Chris Cooley</strong><br/><a href="https://coded.design">coded.design ↗</a> · Open source, MIT</div><a href={repo}>Fork it. Break a page. ↗</a></footer>
  <div class="session-bar" hidden data-virus-ignore data-swarm-ignore><span class="led"></span><strong id="session-name"></strong><span id="session-state">Loading…</span><button id="session-pause">Pause</button><button id="session-recall">Recall <kbd>Esc</kbd></button></div>
  <div id="site-status" class="site-status" role="status" aria-live="polite" data-virus-ignore data-swarm-ignore></div>

</body>
</html>
